""" + TABLE(["Edge (T1 → T2)", "This estimate", "Population target", "Within-person truth", "Share = 0 in bootstrap"], truth_table_rows()) + KEY("Three things to read from this table", "<ol><li>\"No one understands me → Fatigue\", third in the sample ({{V:b10_lone4>dep4_sample}}), is only {{V:pop_lone4>dep4}} in the population, and its within-person truth is {{V:win_lone4>dep4}}. Yet it is quite stable in the bootstrap: only {{V:b10_lone4>dep4_prop0}} of the resamples set it to 0, and the interval [{{V:b10_lone4>dep4_q2.5}}, {{V:b10_lone4>dep4_q97.5}}] {{V:b10_lone4>dep4_cover}} the population value. The bootstrap resamples from the same sample, so it can only tell you how much the estimate fluctuates around this sample; if the sample itself departs from the population, the bootstrap cannot see it.</li><li>The strongest loneliness → depression edge under both kinds of truth is \"Feeling isolated → Depressed mood\" (population {{V:truth_ld_pop}}, within-person {{V:truth_ld_win}}), but this run estimates it at only {{V:truth_ld_est}}.</li><li>The real cross-construct edge of \"No one understands me\" points to \"Worthlessness\" (population {{V:pop_lone4>dep5}}, within-person {{V:win_lone4>dep5}}); this run estimates it at {{V:b10_lone4>dep5_sample}}. The edges from \"No one understands me\" to other loneliness items (→ Feeling left out, → Feeling isolated) are about 0.06 in the population, but their within-person truth is 0: they come from confounding by the stable trait (Lesson 7.5), not from a within-person dynamic process.</li></ol><p>With your own data there is no truth to compare with, and you cannot see biases like these. So base your conclusions on the overall pattern (for example the difference between the edge weights of the two directions, with this difference tested by bootstrap), not on the single most eye-catching edge; and state in the limitations that \"estimates of single edges may have considerable error\".</p>") + r"""
""" + WARN("An important \"contradiction\"", "<p>lone4 (No one understands me) has the highest out-EI, which looks like a \"key intervention target\". But the CS of out-EI is only {{V:cs10_out}}, below the minimum of 0.25: after dropping some participants, the ranking may change. The correct wording: \"lone4 had the highest out-EI in this sample, but the stability of out-EI was insufficient (CS = {{V:cs10_out}}), so this study does not interpret this ranking.\"</p>") + r"""
